# g96-be-tools-a wiring (core)

## native/core/crates/mixdog-app-server-daemon/src/compose_flip.rs
Anchor: the line `tool_pool.push(Arc::new(mixdog_tools::exec::ShellTool::new(b.exec.clone())));` (in the builtin tool pool, after
`mixdog_tools::fs::EditTool::new().with_state(read_state.clone())`).

Replace that one line with these three lines (the shell is shared, so the foreign command names run the same tool):

    let shell = Arc::new(mixdog_tools::exec::ShellTool::new(b.exec.clone()));
    tool_pool.extend(mixdog_tools::exec::ForeignShellTool::of(&shell));
    tool_pool.push(shell);

And add, right after the `EditTool` push line:

    tool_pool.push(Arc::new(mixdog_tools::fs::WriteTool::new().with_state(read_state.clone())));

What this does: `edit` already answers to strreplace / str_replace / str_replace_editor / search_replace (declared aliases on its
spec; mixdog-agent ToolSet now also matches a declared alias in any case). `WriteTool` is the hidden (`public: false`) `write`
tool with aliases create_file / createfile; `ForeignShellTool::of` gives the hidden `bash` (POSIX misuse-guard syntax) and `run`
(aliases runcommand / terminal / run_terminal_cmd). None is in a model-visible tool list. They are in the pool's builtin names
(`ToolSet::new` takes every spec name), so a case variant such as `Bash` / `Write` canonicalizes to them as it did in Node, whose
adapter lower-cased the name; check that no test of the unknown-tool did-you-mean text (mixdog-agent tools / text tests with the
daemon pool) lists `write`, `bash` or `run` as unknown. No SERVICE_PENDING name is removed by this task.

## Protocol rows
None. No new protocol method, notification or type.

## Cargo
None. No new dependency edge (chrono, futures, mixdog-js were already dependencies of mixdog-tools).
